WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.552 --> 00:00:04.682
Buying clothes has never been easier.

00:00:04.682 --> 00:00:08.316
80 billion items are
manufactured every year.

00:00:08.316 --> 00:00:10.083
We're putting too much product out there,

00:00:10.083 --> 00:00:12.569
most of that product
ending up in landfill.

00:00:12.569 --> 00:00:15.269
So-called 'fast fashion' allows consumers

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:18.869
to buy more, but they're wearing
these garments less often

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:22.786
and disposing of them at
an unprecedented rate.

00:00:32.685 --> 00:00:35.935
This is where wardrobe castoffs end up.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.078
Savanna Rags is a clothes recycling

00:00:40.078 --> 00:00:43.745
and processing plant
in Nottingham, England.

00:00:44.971 --> 00:00:47.817
They process discarded
clothes from recycling bins

00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:49.400
around the country.

00:00:50.506 --> 00:00:55.479
Mohammed Patel has been running
this plant for 12 years.

00:00:55.479 --> 00:00:58.550
Majority of it will go
to Africa and Dubai.

00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:05.127
We send some of it to
Europe and we have a couple

00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:09.126
of buyers here in the UK
that buy from us also.

00:01:09.126 --> 00:01:11.517
Globally, sorting plants
like this only deal with

00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:15.213
around 25% of discarded clothes.

00:01:15.213 --> 00:01:18.532
In Britain, more than
300,000 tonnes of clothes

00:01:18.532 --> 00:01:20.951
end up in landfill every year.

00:01:20.951 --> 00:01:24.619
It's the fastest growing
category of waste in the country.

00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:26.396
But this is a global problem.

00:01:26.396 --> 00:01:29.211
Expanding middle classes in
emerging markets are hungry

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:31.448
for more and cheaper fashion.

00:01:31.448 --> 00:01:34.801
It's estimated that by
2050, global clothing sales

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:36.718
could more than triple.

00:01:37.568 --> 00:01:40.844
One of the things that we've
noticed is that the quality

00:01:40.844 --> 00:01:43.115
of the actual material
being used has gone down.

00:01:43.115 --> 00:01:46.062
We're now having to process
a lot more just to get the

00:01:46.062 --> 00:01:49.630
same quality of goods that we can sell on.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.093
But how can the fashion
industry continue to grow

00:01:52.093 --> 00:01:55.544
while addressing the
environmental need for people

00:01:55.544 --> 00:01:57.294
to buy fewer clothes?

00:02:00.301 --> 00:02:04.090
New York fashionista Ijeoma
Kola is less about rags

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.769
and more about the latest runway fashions.

00:02:07.769 --> 00:02:09.955
Because I post often on Instagram,

00:02:09.955 --> 00:02:12.617
there is a little bit of
pressure to have a new outfit.

00:02:17.321 --> 00:02:18.940
She's a fashion blogger.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.044
Her stylish posts and
clothing tips are attracting

00:02:21.044 --> 00:02:23.396
a big online following.

00:02:23.396 --> 00:02:26.557
I found myself before
buying a lot of clothes.

00:02:26.557 --> 00:02:30.303
I usually bought clothes
from H&amp;M and Zara, or ASOS.

00:02:30.303 --> 00:02:32.464
But if you're looking for
trendy pieces they have them,

00:02:32.464 --> 00:02:34.631
they're pretty affordable.

00:02:36.648 --> 00:02:38.650
Today she's looking for
a new outfit for a swanky

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.900
industry event.

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:44.993
But this store doesn't sell
clothes, it rents them.

00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:49.337
Ijeoma has been championing
Rent the Runway's radical

00:02:49.337 --> 00:02:52.307
new approach to high end fashion.

00:02:52.307 --> 00:02:54.962
Rent the Runway is a
clothing borrowing service

00:02:54.962 --> 00:02:58.495
which allows you to rent
clothes for either 4 or 8 days

00:02:58.495 --> 00:03:00.014
at a time.

00:03:00.014 --> 00:03:01.803
You are cycling through clothing as fast

00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:03.746
but you're borrowing it with other people

00:03:03.746 --> 00:03:05.456
so other people get to wear the same thing

00:03:05.456 --> 00:03:07.413
that you're wearing as well.

00:03:07.413 --> 00:03:09.753
On average only 20% of clothes are worn

00:03:09.753 --> 00:03:12.116
on a regular basis.

00:03:12.116 --> 00:03:14.670
Rent the Runway's mission
is to change consumers

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.708
relationship with the clothes they wear.

00:03:17.708 --> 00:03:19.980
Rather than buying something
only getting to wear it

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.174
maybe three or four times

00:03:21.174 --> 00:03:24.447
before you decide to give
it away or throw it away

00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:27.518
an item is worn a lot more
when it is being shared

00:03:27.518 --> 00:03:29.518
across different people.

00:03:31.006 --> 00:03:34.145
Rent the Runway only has a
couple of flagship stores

00:03:34.145 --> 00:03:37.745
but online it's a giant
and it's disrupting

00:03:37.745 --> 00:03:39.590
the fashion industry.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.335
To date, there are 10 million
members so it comes with

00:03:42.335 --> 00:03:44.085
a hefty laundry bill.

00:03:45.148 --> 00:03:47.819
The company claims to have the
largest dry cleaning facility

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:48.902
in the world.

00:03:49.933 --> 00:03:52.026
Rent the Runway is getting
more mileage out of

00:03:52.026 --> 00:03:54.760
items of clothing, it's
also helping to tackle

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.956
an increasing throwaway culture.

00:03:57.956 --> 00:03:59.868
But the last thing clothing brands want

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:03.018
is for consumers to buy less.

00:04:03.018 --> 00:04:06.956
Except perhaps, for Patagonia,
an outdoor apparel brand

00:04:06.956 --> 00:04:09.307
which sent shock waves
through the industry with this

00:04:09.307 --> 00:04:11.670
full page advert in the New York Times

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.420
on Black Friday 2011.

00:04:15.517 --> 00:04:18.162
Here in Amsterdam, Ryan
Gellert heads up Patagonia's

00:04:18.162 --> 00:04:20.837
operations in Europe and the Middle East.

00:04:25.499 --> 00:04:27.884
The apparel industry has become

00:04:27.884 --> 00:04:29.798
one of the most polluting in the world

00:04:29.798 --> 00:04:33.667
as an industry we're creating
product that people don't need

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:36.266
by stimulating demand
and creating this sense

00:04:36.266 --> 00:04:39.551
that if you don't buy it now
it's not going to be available.

00:04:39.551 --> 00:04:43.252
There's this race to the
bottom on price and quality

00:04:43.252 --> 00:04:46.166
that is an unsustainable model.

00:04:46.166 --> 00:04:49.913
Patagonia's philosophy flies
in the face of fast fashion

00:04:49.913 --> 00:04:52.861
to buy once, buy well, and mend clothing

00:04:52.861 --> 00:04:54.964
for a longer lifespan.

00:04:54.964 --> 00:04:57.237
So maybe doing that in
Amsterdam and then figuring out

00:04:57.237 --> 00:04:59.433
how to share it elsewhere.

00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:02.785
With the largest single repair
facility in North America,

00:05:02.785 --> 00:05:06.498
and mobile mending services
around Europe and America,

00:05:06.498 --> 00:05:09.254
Patagonia's anti-fashion
environmental message

00:05:09.254 --> 00:05:12.978
has resonated with people
who buy into their vision.

00:05:12.978 --> 00:05:15.645
Helping our customers keep
their product in use longer

00:05:15.645 --> 00:05:19.978
was also one of the original
big ideas in Patagonia.

00:05:21.866 --> 00:05:26.199
Between 2008 and 2014,
profits reportedly tripled.

00:05:27.817 --> 00:05:30.383
Patagonia claims it
generates revenue of nearly

00:05:30.383 --> 00:05:32.466
1 billion dollars a year.

00:05:35.344 --> 00:05:37.684
It's hope is to inspire
other brands to tackle the

00:05:37.684 --> 00:05:40.587
environmental impact of fast fashion.

00:05:40.587 --> 00:05:43.535
If I had the opportunity to sit down with

00:05:43.535 --> 00:05:46.539
leaders from some of the
bigger fast fashion companies

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:49.632
in the world, what I'd really
encourage them to understand

00:05:49.632 --> 00:05:52.738
the full impact of their supply chains.

00:05:52.738 --> 00:05:55.291
Patagonia provides a glimpse
into a more enlightened

00:05:55.291 --> 00:05:56.889
approach to fashion.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.139
But they're a rare example.

00:06:01.772 --> 00:06:04.449
For Mohammed, the boom in
fast fashion has been good

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:07.059
for business, but this throw away culture

00:06:07.059 --> 00:06:09.817
sits uncomfortably with him.

00:06:09.817 --> 00:06:11.814
Sometimes it's soul destroying,
because you come to work

00:06:11.814 --> 00:06:13.773
and you just think, is
this what we've come to

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:18.103
that the human race all we think
about is dispose of things.

00:06:18.103 --> 00:06:22.010
The environmental impact on
the planet is just colossal

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.834
and I don't think we as
the human race realize

00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:27.478
what it is that we're doing,
just for the sake of wearing

00:06:27.478 --> 00:06:29.436
a pair of jeans.

00:06:29.436 --> 00:06:31.753
In order to tackle the throwaway culture,

00:06:31.753 --> 00:06:35.052
brands and consumers need
to change their behaviors.

00:06:35.052 --> 00:06:37.739
Industry pioneers are
proving that there are viable

00:06:37.739 --> 00:06:40.607
business opportunities in selling less,

00:06:40.607 --> 00:06:42.857
others need to follow suit.

